## Why This Is Asked
This probes multi-tenant governance, scale, streaming semantics, schema drift handling, and retention policies across a large lakehouse. It also tests trade-offs between Spark and Flink for high-volume streaming and the practicality of per-tenant partitioning and access control.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant data contracts and lineage
- Exactly-once streaming and idempotent upserts
- Iceberg partitioning by tenant/region
- Schema drift handling and canonical schema
- Data retention, GDPR-style deletes, and privacy masking

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: validate event against per-tenant contract before upsert
function process(event, tenant) {
  const contract = registry.get(tenant);
  if (!contract.validate(event)) throw new Error('Contract violation');
  iceberg.merge(event, canonicalKey, tenant);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test contract drift across tenants over time?
- What metrics and alerts would you monitor for data quality and lineage?

## Why This Is Asked
Design a scalable, auditable CDC ingestion that evolves schemas safely while preserving privacy and lineage.

## Key Concepts
- Central schema registry with versioned contracts
- Idempotent upserts and MERGE semantics
- Dynamic schema evolution and tenant isolation
- Late data buffering and privacy-preserving BI views
- End-to-end testing and monitoring of contracts and lineage

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: contract validation and upsert
function upsertEvent(event, registry) {
  const contract = registry.getContract(event.version, event.schemaKey);
  if (!contract.validate(event)) throw new Error('Contract violation');
  // perform idempotent merge into Iceberg
  mergeIntoIceberg(event, contract.primaryKey);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test contract drift in production and implement safe rollback?
- How would you monitor per-tenant data isolation and lineage end-to-end?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a simple, robust ingestion path with per-record contracts, schema normalization, and safe upserts, plus practical validation and monitoring.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight schema registry for canonical schema
- Per-record contracts via metadata and record_hash
- Upserts with MERGE into Delta Lake
- Bad_data routing for invalid records
- Late data handling with watermarking
- Basic monitoring and lineage capture

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-Spark-like sketch in JS for readability
const raw = readJsonFeeds(regions);
const canonical = raw.map(r => normalize(r));
canonical.forEach(rec => {
  rec._region = rec.region;
  rec._ingest_ts = Date.now();
  rec._record_hash = sha256(JSON.stringify(rec));
});
writeDeltaLake(canonical, 'db.delta_table');
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you evolve the schema registry to handle drift?
- How would you test deduplication guarantees in CI/CD?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests privacy-first, scalable analytics across regions with data contracts.

## Key Concepts

- Per-region pseudonymization
- Iceberg upserts
- Event-time windows and lateness handling
- Immutable audits and data contracts
- Spark vs Flink trade-offs for streaming

## Code Example

```javascript
// Region-aware salt hash (Node.js)
const crypto = require('crypto');
function saltedHash(region, id, salts){
  const salt = salts[region] ?? salts.default;
  return crypto.createHash('sha256').update(id + salt).digest('hex');
}
```

